oindent Since $\Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_\infty \leqslant \varepsilon$, we certainly have that $$\left\vert b_1 n_1 + b_2n_2 + \sum\limits_{j=3}^d n_j\sum\limits_{i=1}^m a_i \lambda_{ij}ight\vert \ll \varepsilon,$$ and then (ef{the critical observation for construction}) follows by the triangle inequality and the fact that $\eta N\geqslant \varepsilon$.\ The constraint (ef{the critical observation for construction}) will turn out to be enough for the proof. We consider various cases, constructing different counterexample functions $f_1$ and $f_2$ based on the size and sign of $b_1$ and $b_2$. To facilitate this, we let $c_1$ be a suitably small positive constant, depending on $c$ and $C$, but independent of $\eta$. All constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ to follow will be assumed to satisfy $C_1,C_2 = O_{c,C}(1)$. \\ 
oindent 	extbf{Case 1: $\vert b_1\vert,\vert b_2\vert \leqslant c_1$.} Under the assumptions of Theorem ef{Converse to main theorem}, this case is actually precluded. Indeed, consider the matrix $L^{\prime\prime}$, defined by taking $$\lambda_{ij}^{\prime\prime} = \lambda_{ij}^\prime$$ for all pairs $(i.j)\in [m]	imes[d]$, except for $(1,1)$ and $(1,2)$. In these cases we let  egin{align*} \lambda_{11}^{\prime\prime} &= \lambda_{11}^\prime -  rac{b_1}{a_1}\ \lambda_{12}^{\prime\prime} & = \lambda_{12}^\prime -  rac{b_2}{a_1}. \end{align*} Then $$\sum\limits_{i=1}^m a_i\lambda_{ij}^{\prime\prime} = 0$$ for all $j$ in the range $1\leqslant j\leqslant d$. In other words we have shown that $\Vert L-L^{\prime\prime}\Vert_{\infty} \leqslant \eta + c_1$ for some matrix $L^{\prime\prime}$ with rank less than $m$. Since $\eta + c_1< c$ (if $c_1$ is small enough), this implies that $\operatorname{dist}(L,V_{\operatorname{rank}}(m,d))<c$, which contradicts the assumptions of Theorem ef{Converse to main theorem}. Therefore this case is indeed precluded. \ 
oindent 	extbf{Case 2: $b_1,b_2$ both of the same sign, and $b_1,b_2\geqslant c_1$.} In this case, (ef{the critical observation for construction}) implies ootnote{The same conclusion is true for $n_2$, but this will not be needed.} that $n_1\leqslant C_1\eta N$ for some constant $C_1$. Now, define $f_1:[N]\longrightarrow [-1,1]$ to be the indicator function of the interval $[\lceil C_1\eta Nceil, N]\cap \mathbb{N}$. We then have  egin{align*} \Vert f_1 - 1\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]} &\ll \Big( rac{1}{N^{s+2}} \sum\limits_{x,h_1,\dots,h_{s+1}\ll C_1\eta N} 1\Big)^{ rac{1}{2^{s+1}}}\ &\leqslant C_2(C_1\eta)^{ rac{s+2}{2^{s+1}}} \end{align*} 
oindent for some constant $C_2$. However, observe that  egin{align*} \vert T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1-1,1,\dots,1)\vert &= \vert T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,1,\dots,1) - T_{F,G,N}^L(1,1,\dots,1)\vert\ & = \vert 0 - T_{F,G,N}^L(1,1,\dots,1)\vert \gg_{c,C,\varepsilon} 1 \end{align*} 
oindent by the hypotheses of Theorem ef{Converse to main theorem}. If $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1 - 1,1,\dots,1)$ did not satisfy (ef{conclusion repeated}), then $$1 \ll_{c,C,\varepsilon} H(ho) + E_{ho}(1),$$ where $ho := C_2(C_1\eta)^{ rac{s+2}{2^{s+1}}}$. Picking $\eta$ small enough, then $N$ large enough, this inequality cannot possibly hold, and we have a contradiction. So $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1 - 1,1,\dots,1)$ satisfies (ef{conclusion repeated}). \ 
oindent 	extbf{Case 3: $b_1,b_2$ of opposite signs, and $b_1,b_2\geqslant c_1$.} This is the most involved case, although the central idea is very simple. The condition (ef{the critical observation for construction}) confines $n_2$ to lie within a certain distance of a fixed multiple of $n_1$. By constructing functions $f_1$ and $f_2$ using random choices of blocks of this length, but coupled in such a way that condition (ef{the critical observation for construction}) is very likely to hold, we can guarantee that $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1-p,f_2-p,1,\dots,1)$ is bounded away from zero, where $p$ is the probability used to choose the random blocks. However, despite the block construction and the coupling, the functions $f_1$ and $f_2$ still individually exhibit enough randomness to conclude that $\Vert f_1 - p\Vert _{U^{s+1}[N]} = o(1)$ as $Nightarrow \infty$, and the same for $f_2$. \ We now fill in the technical details. Relation (ef{the critical observation for construction}) implies that  egin{equation} \vert b_1n_1 + b_2n_2\vert \leqslant C_1\eta N, \end{equation} 
