oindent where $\omega_V \in \operatorname{span}(\mathbf{x_i^*}:i\leqslant u)$, $\omega_W \in \operatorname{span}(\Xi(\mathbf{w_j})^*:j\leqslant d-u)$, and $\Vert\omega_W\Vert_\infty = O_{c,C}(\eta)$. But $L^*P^* \pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi) \in \operatorname{span}(\Xi(\mathbf{w_j})^*:j\leqslant d-u)$ too. Indeed, for every $i$ at most $d-u$, \[L^*P^* \pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi)(\mathbf{x_i}) = \varphi (\pi_{m-u} PL\mathbf{x_i}) = \varphi(\mathbf{0}) = 0,\] by the properties of $P$ (see (ef{equation properties of P})). Therefore $\omega_V = \mathbf{0}$, and so \[\Vert L^*P^* \pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi) - \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \lambda_j \Xi(\mathbf{w_j}) ^*\Vert_\infty = O_{c,C}(\eta).\] Since $\sum_{j=1}^{d-u} \lambda_j \Xi(\mathbf{w_j}) ^* \in (\mathbb{Z}^d)^T$, this implies (ef{first approximation equation of part 8 of the lemma}) as claimed.\ The bound (ef{second approximation equation of part 8 of the lemma}) is immediate from the bounds on the coefficients of $P^*$ and $\pi_{m-u}^*$, so it remains to prove (ef{third approximation equation of part 8 of the lemma}). Suppose for contradiction that, for some small parameter $\delta$, \[P^*\pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi) = \alpha_1 + \alpha_2,\] where $\alpha_1 \in \Theta^*((\mathbb{R}^{u})^*)$ and $\Vert \alpha_2 \Vert_\infty \leqslant \delta 	au_1$. We know that $\Vert \varphi \Vert_\infty \geqslant 	au_1$, which means that there is some standard basis vector $\mathbf{f_k} \in \mathbb{R}^{m-u}$ for which $\vert \varphi(\mathbf{f_k})\vert \geqslant 	au_1$. Let $\mathbf{b_{k+u}}$ be the standard basis vector of $\mathbb{R}^m$ for which $\pi_{m-u}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}}) = \mathbf{f_k}$. Recall the properties of $P$ (given in (ef{equation properties of P}) and (ef{equation properties of P 2})), in particular recall that $P:\ker \Theta \longrightarrow \{0\}^u 	imes \mathbb{R}^{m-u}$ is an isomorphism. Then \[\vert P^* \pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi)(P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}}))\vert = \vert\pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi)(\mathbf{b_{k+u}})\vert = \vert \varphi(\mathbf{f_k})\vert \geqslant 	au_1.\] Note that $\Theta^*((\mathbb{R}^{u})^*) = (\ker \Theta)^{0}$, and so \[\vert P^* \pi_{m-u}^*(\varphi)(P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}}))\vert = \vert(\alpha_1 + \alpha_2)(P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}}))\vert = \vert \alpha_2(P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}})) \vert \ll_{c,C} \delta 	au_1,\] as $P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}}) \in \ker \Theta$ and satisfies $\Vert P^{-1}(\mathbf{b_{k+u}})\Vert_\infty = O_{c,C}(1)$. This is a contradiction if $\delta$ is small enough, and so (ef{third approximation equation of part 8 of the lemma}) holds. This resolves part (8).\

	extbf{Part (9)}: Let $	au_1,	au_2 \in (0,1]$. We desire to prove the relationship  egin{equation} A_{L^\prime}(	au_1,	au_2) \ll_{c,C} A_L(\Omega_{c,C}(	au_1),\Omega_{c,C}(	au_2)), \end{equation} 
oindent where $L^\prime$ is as in (ef{equation definition of L prime}).This inequality is the reverse inequality of part (8), and in fact it will not be required in the proof of any of our main theorems. However, it will be required in order to analyse $A_L(	au_1,	au_2)$ when $L$ has algebraic coefficients (in Appendix ef{section algebraic approximation}), so we choose to state and prove it here, close to our argument for part (8). Suppose that $A_L(	au_1,	au_2)<\eta$, for some parameter $\eta$. Then there exists some $\varphi\in (\mathbb{R}^m)^*$ such that $\operatorname{dist}(\varphi, \Theta^*((\mathbb{R}^u)^*)) \geqslant 	au_1$, $\Vert \varphi \Vert_\infty \leqslant 	au_2^{-1}$, and $\operatorname{dist}(L^*\varphi, (\mathbb{Z}^d)^T) < \eta$. So there exists some $\omega \in (\mathbb{Z}^d)^T$ for which \[ \Vert L^* \varphi - \omega\Vert_\infty < \eta.\] We expand both $L^*\varphi$ and $\omega$ with respect to the dual basis $\mathcal{B}^*$ from (ef{funky dual basis}). So,  egin{align*} L^*\varphi &= \sum\limits_{i=1}^u \lambda_i \mathbf{x_i^*} + \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \mu_j \Xi(\mathbf{w_j})^*\ \omega& = \sum\limits_{i=1}^u \lambda_i^\prime \mathbf{x_i^*} + \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \mu_j^\prime \Xi(\mathbf{w_j})^*. \end{align*} 
oindent Since $\mathcal{B}^*$ is a lattice basis for $(\mathbb{Z}^d)^T$, we have $\lambda_i^\prime \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $\mu_j^\prime \in\mathbb{Z}$ for each $i$ and $j$. Since the change of basis matrix between $\mathcal{B}^*$ and the standard dual basis has integer coefficients that are bounded in absolute value by $O_{c,C}(1)$ (part (2) of Lemma ef{Lemma dual space decomposition}), one has $\vert \lambda_i - \lambda_i^\prime\vert = O_{c,C}(\eta)$ and $\vert \mu_j - \mu_j^\prime\vert = O_{c,C}(\eta)$ for each $i$ and $j$. Let $\mathbf{w_1^*}, \dots,\mathbf{w_{d-u}^*}$ denote the standard dual basis of $(\mathbb{R}^{d-u})^*$, and define \[\omega^\prime: = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \mu_j^\prime \mathbf{w_j^*}.\] Certainly $\omega^\prime \in (\mathbb{Z}^{d-u})^T$. We claim that there exists a map $\varphi^\prime \in (\mathbb{R}^{m-u})^*$ such that $	au_1 \ll_{c,C} \Vert \varphi^\prime \Vert_\infty \ll_{c,C} 	au_2^{-1}$ and $\Vert (L^\prime)^* \varphi^\prime - \omega^\prime\Vert_\infty \ll_{c,C} \eta$, which will immediately resolve (ef{claim of part 9}) and part (9). \ Indeed, recall the decomposition $\mathbb{R}^m = (\operatorname{span}(L\mathbf{x_i}: i\leqslant u)) \oplus \ker \Theta$ as an algebraic direct sum from (ef{direct sum}). Let $\varphi = \varphi_1 + \varphi_2$, where $\varphi_1 \in(\operatorname{span}(L\mathbf{x_i}: i\leqslant u))^{0} $ and $\varphi_2 \in (\ker \Theta)^{0}.$ Since $\operatorname{dist}(\varphi, (\ker \Theta)^{0}) \geqslant 	au_1$, we have $\Vert \varphi_1\Vert_\infty \geqslant 	au_1$. By the properties of the matrix $P$ ((ef{equation properties of P}) and (ef{equation properties of P 2})) there exists some $\varphi^\prime \in (\mathbb{R}^{m-u})^*$ such that \[\varphi_1 = P^* \pi_{m-u}^* \varphi^\prime.\] Furthermore, by evaluating $\varphi^\prime$ at the standard basis vectors, one sees that \[ 	au_1 \ll_{c,C} \Vert \varphi^\prime \Vert_\infty \ll_{c,C} 	au_2^{-1}.\] We shall use this $\varphi^\prime$. By evaluating $L^*\varphi_1$ at the elements of $\mathcal{B}$ one immediately sees that \[L^*\varphi_1 = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \mu_j \Xi(\mathbf{w_j})^*.\] Hence \[\Xi^*L^*P^* \pi_{m-u}^* \varphi^\prime = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{d-u} \mu_j \mathbf{w_j^*},\] in other words $(L^\prime)^* \varphi^\prime = \sum_{j=1}^{d-u}\mu_j \mathbf{w_j^*}.$ But since $\vert \mu_j - \mu_j^\prime\vert = O_{c,C}(\eta)$ for each $j$, one has $\Vert (L^\prime)^* \varphi^\prime - \omega^\prime\Vert_\infty = O_{c,C} (\eta)$ as required. This settles part (9). \ 
oindent The entire lemma is settled.

	extbf{Case 2: $b_1,b_2$ both of the same sign, and $b_1,b_2\geqslant c_1$.} In this case, (ef{the critical observation for construction}) implies ootnote{The same conclusion is true for $n_2$, but this will not be needed.} that $n_1\leqslant C_1\eta N$ for some constant $C_1$. Now, define $f_1:[N]\longrightarrow [-1,1]$ to be the indicator function of the interval $[\lceil C_1\eta Nceil, N]\cap \mathbb{N}$. We then have  egin{align*} \Vert f_1 - 1\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]} &\ll \Big( rac{1}{N^{s+2}} \sum\limits_{x,h_1,\dots,h_{s+1}\ll C_1\eta N} 1\Big)^{ rac{1}{2^{s+1}}}\ &\leqslant C_2(C_1\eta)^{ rac{s+2}{2^{s+1}}} \end{align*} 
oindent for some constant $C_2$. However, observe that  egin{align*} \vert T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1-1,1,\dots,1)\vert &= \vert T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,1,\dots,1) - T_{F,G,N}^L(1,1,\dots,1)\vert\ & = \vert 0 - T_{F,G,N}^L(1,1,\dots,1)\vert \gg_{c,C,\varepsilon} 1 \end{align*} 
oindent by the hypotheses of Theorem ef{Converse to main theorem}. If $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1 - 1,1,\dots,1)$ did not satisfy (ef{conclusion repeated}), then $$1 \ll_{c,C,\varepsilon} H(ho) + E_{ho}(1),$$ where $ho := C_2(C_1\eta)^{ rac{s+2}{2^{s+1}}}$. Picking $\eta$ small enough, then $N$ large enough, this inequality cannot possibly hold, and we have a contradiction. So $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1 - 1,1,\dots,1)$ satisfies (ef{conclusion repeated}). \ 
oindent

	extbf{Case 3: $b_1,b_2$ of opposite signs, and $b_1,b_2\geqslant c_1$.} This is the most involved case, although the central idea is very simple. The condition (ef{the critical observation for construction}) confines $n_2$ to lie within a certain distance of a fixed multiple of $n_1$. By constructing functions $f_1$ and $f_2$ using random choices of blocks of this length, but coupled in such a way that condition (ef{the critical observation for construction}) is very likely to hold, we can guarantee that $T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1-p,f_2-p,1,\dots,1)$ is bounded away from zero, where $p$ is the probability used to choose the random blocks. However, despite the block construction and the coupling, the functions $f_1$ and $f_2$ still individually exhibit enough randomness to conclude that $\Vert f_1 - p\Vert _{U^{s+1}[N]} = o(1)$ as $Nightarrow \infty$, and the same for $f_2$. \ We now fill in the technical details. Relation (ef{the critical observation for construction}) implies that  egin{equation} \vert b_1n_1 + b_2n_2\vert \leqslant C_1\eta N, \end{equation} 
oindent for some $C_1$ satisfying $C_1 = O(1)$, and without loss of generality assume that $b_1$ is positive, $b_2$ is negative, and $\vert b_1\vert$ is at least $\vert b_2\vert$. Let $C_2$ be some parameter, chosen so that $(C_1C_2\eta)^{-1}$ is an integer. Such a $C_2$ will of course depend on $\eta$, but in magnitude we may pick $C_2\asymp 1$. We consider the real interval $[0,N]$ modulo $N$, and for $x\in [0,N]$ and $i$ in the range $0\leqslant i\leqslant (C_1C_2\eta)^{-1} - 1$ we define the half-open interval modulo $N$ $$I_{x,i}: = [x+ i C_1C_2\eta N,x+(i+1)C_1C_2\eta N).$$ This choice guarantees that  egin{equation} [0,N] =  igcup\limits_{i=0}^{(C_1C_2\eta)^{-1} - 1} I_{x,i}, \end{equation} and the union is disjoint. Now, for $\delta$ a small constant to be chosen later ootnote{This $\delta$ is unrelated to the notation $\delta = T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,\dots,f_d)$ used in previous sections.}, we define $$I^\delta_{x,i}: = [x+ (i+ rac{1}{2}-\delta) C_1C_2\eta N,x+(i+ rac{1}{2}+\delta)C_1C_2\eta N).$$ We will use the partition (ef{the defining partition}) to construct a function $f_1$, using an averaging argument to choose an $x$ so that the $I_{x,i}^\delta$ intervals capture a positive proportion of the solution density of the linear inequality system. Indeed, for $n_1\in [N]$ let the weight $u(n_1)$ denote the number of $d-1$-tuples $n_2,\dots,n_d\leqslant N$ that together with $n_1$ satisfy the inequality $\Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_\infty < \varepsilon$. The weight $u(n_1)$ could be zero, of course. Let \[E_{x,\delta} := \cup_i I_{x,i}^\delta.\] Then  egin{align*}  rac{1}{N}\int\limits_{0}^N \sum\limits_{n\in [N]} u(n) 1_{E_{x,\delta}}(n) \, dx & =  rac{1}{N}\sum\limits_{n\in [N]} u(n)\int\limits_{0}^N 1_{E_{x,\delta}}(n)\, dx \ & = \sum\limits_{n\in [N]} u(n) 2\delta \ & = 2\delta N^{d-m}T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1) \end{align*} 
oindent Therefore, by the assumptions of Theorem ef{Converse to main theorem}, we may fix an $x$ such that  egin{equation} \sum\limits_{n\in [N]} u(n) 1_{E_{x,\delta}}(n) \gg_{c,C} \delta N^{d-m}T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1). \end{equation} Let us finally define the function $f_1$. Let $p$ be a small positive constant (to be decided later). Fix a value of $x$ such that (ef{capturing solutions in intervals}) holds. Then we define a random subset $A\subseteq[N]$ by picking all of $I_{x,i} \cap \mathbb{N}$ to be members of $A$, with probability $p$, or none of $I_{x,i} \cap \mathbb{N}$ to be members of $A$, with probability $1-p$. We then make this same choice for each $i$ in the range $0\leqslant i \leqslant (C_1C_2\eta)^{-1} - 1$, independently. Observe immediately that for each $n\in [N]$ the probability that $n\in A$ is always $p$ (though these events are not always independent). We let $f_1(n)$ be the indicator function $1_A(n)$. The function $f_2$ is defined in terms of $f_1$. Indeed, let $$J_{x,i} =  rac{b_1}{\vert b_2\vert} I_{x,i}\cap (0,N],$$ where the dilation of the interval $I_{x,i}$ is not considered modulo $N$ but rather just as an operator on subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ (see Section ef{Section Notation} for this notation). Since $b_1\geqslant \vert b_2\vert$ we have that these $J_{x,i}$ also form a disjoint partition of $[0,N]$. [NB: If $b_1>\vert b_2\vert$ it may be that certain $J_{x,i}$ are empty, since the dilate of the corresponding $I_{x,i}$ may land entirely outside $[0,N]$.] Then let $B$ be the subset of $[N]$ defined so that for each $i$ with $J_{x,i}$ non-empty we have $J_{x,i}\cap \mathbb{N}\subseteq B$ if and only if $I_{x,i}\cap \mathbb{N}\subseteq A$. Note again that for each individual $n\in [N]$ the probability that $n\in B$ is always $p$. We let $f_2(n)$ be the indicator function $1_B(n)$. \ Our first claim is that, if $p$ is small enough in terms of $\delta$,  egin{equation} \vert \mathbb{E} T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,f_2,1,\dots,1)-T_{F,G,N}^L(p,p,1,\dots,1)\vert \gg_{c,C,\varepsilon} \delta^2. \end{equation} Indeed, suppose that $I_{x,i}$ is included in the set $A$, and suppose that $n_1\in I_{x,i}^\delta$. If $n_2\in [N]$ satisfies $\vert  rac{b_1}{\vert b_2\vert} n_1 - n_2\vert \leqslant  rac{1}{b_2}C_1\eta N$ and if $\delta$ is small enough in terms of $b_1$ and $b_2$, then ootnote{This fact is the reason why we introduced the parameter $\delta$.} $n_2 \in J_{x,i}$. Thus, by the observation (ef{the critical equation for construction with an explicit constant}), $n_2 \in B$, for every integer $n_2$ that is the second coordinate of a solution vector ootnote{i.e a vector $\mathbf{n}$ such that $\Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_\infty \leqslant \varepsilon$.} $\mathbf{n}$ for which the first coordinate is $n_1$. Therefore  egin{align*} \mathbb{E}T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,f_2,1,\dots,1)& =  rac{1}{N^{d-m}}\sum\limits_{\substack{\mathbf{n}\in [N]^d \ \Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_{\infty} \leqslant\varepsilon}} \mathbb{P}(n_1\in A\wedge n_2\in B)\ &\geqslant  rac{1}{N^{d-m}}\sum\limits_{\substack{\mathbf{n}\in [N]^d \ \Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_{\infty} \leqslant \varepsilon}} \mathbb{P}(n_1\in A \wedge n_1 \in I_{x,i}^\delta 	ext{ for some } i \wedge n_2\in B)\ &\geqslant  rac{1}{N^{d-m}}\sum\limits_{\substack{\mathbf{n}\in [N]^d \ \Vert L\mathbf{n}\Vert_{\infty} \leqslant \varepsilon}} \mathbb{P}(n_1\in A \wedge n_1 \in I_{x,i}^\delta 	ext{ for some } i)\ & =  rac{1}{N^{d-m}}\sum\limits_{n_1\in [N] } u(n_1)p1_{E_{x,\delta}} (n_1)\ &\geqslant 2\delta p T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1), \end{align*} 
oindent where the final line follows from (ef{capturing solutions in intervals}). On the other hand $T_{F,G,N}^L(p,p,1,\dots,1) = p^2T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1)$, and hence  egin{equation} \mathbb{E} T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,f_2,1,\dots,1)-T_{F,G,N}^L(p,p,1,\dots,1)\geqslant (2\delta p - p^2)T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1). \end{equation} Picking $p$ small enough in terms of $\delta$, and using the assumption that $T_{F,G,N}^L(1,\dots,1) = \Omega_{c,C,\varepsilon}(1)$, this proves the relation (ef{lower bound on solution count of coupled random sets}). \ Our second claim is that  egin{equation} \mathbb{E}\Vert f_1 - p\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]},\mathbb{E}\Vert f_2 - p\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]} \ll \eta^{ rac{1}{2^{s+1}}}. \end{equation} We first consider $f_1$. Then $$\mathbb{E}\Vert f_1 - p\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]}^{2^{s+1}} \ll  rac{1}{N^{s+2}}\sum\limits_{\substack(x,\mathbf{h})\in \mathbb{Z}^{s+2}}\ \mathbb{E}\Big(\prod\limits_{ oldsymbol{\omega} \in \{0,1\}^{s+1}} (f_1 - p1_{[N]})(x+\mathbf{h}\cdot oldsymbol{\omega})\Big).$$ Observe that for fixed $(x,\mathbf{h})$ the random variables $(f_1 - p1_{[N]})(x+\mathbf{h}\cdot oldsymbol{\omega})$ each have mean zero and, unless some two of the expressions $x+\mathbf{h}\cdot oldsymbol{\omega}$ lie in the same block $I_i$, these random variables are independent. Hence, apart from those exceptional cases, we may factor the expectation and conclude that $$\mathbb{E}\Big(\prod\limits_{ oldsymbol{\omega} \in \{0,1\}^{s+1}} (f_1 - p1_{[N]})(x+\mathbf{h}\cdot oldsymbol{\omega})\Big) = \prod\limits_{ oldsymbol{\omega} \in \{0,1\}^{s+1}} \mathbb{E}((f_1 - p1_{[N]})(x+\mathbf{h}\cdot oldsymbol{\omega})) = 0.$$ Therefore,  egin{align*} \mathbb{E}\Vert f_1 - p\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]}^{2^{s+1}}&\ll rac{1}{N^{s+2}} \sum\limits_{(x,\mathbf{h})\in [-N,N]^{s+2}} 1_R(\mathbf{h})\ &\ll \eta, \end{align*} 
oindent where \[R = \{\mathbf{h}:\vert\mathbf{h}\cdot( oldsymbol{\omega_1} -  oldsymbol{\omega_2})\vert \leqslant C_1C_2\eta N 	ext{ for some } oldsymbol{\omega_1}, oldsymbol{\omega_2}\in \{0,1\}^{s+1},\,  oldsymbol{\omega_1}
eq oldsymbol{\omega_2}\}.\] 
oindent Thus by Jensen's inequality we have  egin{equation} \mathbb{E}\Vert f_1 - p\Vert_{U^{s+1}[N]} \ll \eta^{ rac{1}{2^{s+1}}}, \end{equation} 
oindent as claimed in (ef{equatoin bound on expectation of gowers norms}). The calculation for $f_2$ is essentially identical, noting that the length of the blocks $J_{x,i}$ is also $O(\eta N)$. \ It is possible that one could finish the argument here by considering a second moment, and choosing some explicit $f_1$ and $f_2$. To avoid calculating a second moment, we argue as follows. Suppose for contradiction that there were no functions $f_1,\dots,f_d$ that satisfied (ef{conclusion repeated}). Then, by (ef{lower bound on solution count of coupled random sets}), if we pick $p$ to be small enough in terms of $\delta$ we have  egin{align} \delta^2 &\ll_{c,C,\varepsilon} \vert \mathbb{E}T_{F,G,N}^L(f_1,f_2,1,\dots,1) - T_{F,G,N}^L(p,p,1,\dots,1)\vert 
